<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · sea</p><h1 class="enc-title">Atlantic Ocean</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Atlantic</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Atlantic Ocean separates <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>. From the fifteenth century it became the main highway of European colonial expansion, used by the Portuguese, Spanish, British and French empires. It carried the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">Atlantic slave trade</a>, which transformed African states such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-kongo">Kongo</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oyo-empire">Oyo</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-empire">Benin</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashanti-empire">Ashanti</a> empires, and in antiquity it marked the outer limit of Punic voyages.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/atlantic-ocean.jpg" alt="Atlantic Ocean" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CIA · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Atlantic_Ocean_-_en.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Atlantic is the second largest of the world's five oceans, with an area of about 85,133,000 square kilometres, roughly 17% of Earth's surface. It occupies an elongated, S-shaped basin between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> to the east and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a> to the west. It connects to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arctic-ocean">Arctic Ocean</a> in the north, the Pacific in the southwest, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-ocean">Indian Ocean</a> in the southeast along the 20° East meridian, and the Southern Ocean in the south. The Equator divides it into northern and southern parts.</p><p>A submarine mountain range, the Mid-Atlantic Ridge, runs down its length from 87°N to Bouvet Island at 54°S and divides it into two halves. It marks the boundary between the North American and Eurasian plates and between the South American and African plates. The break-up of Pangaea began in the central Atlantic, between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a> and Northwest Africa, in the Late Triassic and Early Jurassic. The Gulf Stream and the North Atlantic Drift help keep the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-isles">British Isles</a> and northwestern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> mild in winter.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The oldest known mentions of an Atlantic sea come from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stesichorus">Stesichorus</a> around the mid-sixth century BCE and from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> around 450 BCE, where the name refers to the sea beyond the Pillars of Hercules, named for the Titan Atlas. Early Greek sailors believed it to be part of Oceanus, the great sea or river that surrounds all land. The southern Atlantic was called the Aethiopian Ocean as late as the mid-19th century, and English cartographers of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/age-of-discovery">Age of Discovery</a> called it the Great Western Ocean.</p><p>The Norse were the first known humans to cross the ocean, but the expedition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christopher-columbus">Christopher Columbus</a> in 1492 proved the most consequential. It began an age of exploration and colonisation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a> by European powers, most notably Portugal, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and the United Kingdom.</p><p>From the 16th to the 19th centuries the Atlantic was the centre of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">Atlantic slave trade</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/columbian-exchange">Columbian exchange</a>, and it occasionally hosted naval battles. Such battles and the growing trade of American powers such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a> increased in the early 20th century. Notable postwar conflicts include the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuban-missile-crisis">Cuban Missile Crisis</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/falklands-war">Falklands War</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The ocean remains a core component of trade around the world, with major transatlantic transport and communication routes. Its continental shelves hold petroleum and gas fields. They also include some of the world's richest fishing grounds, among them the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-banks-of-newfoundland">Grand Banks of Newfoundland</a>, Georges Bank and the Dogger Bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-sea">North Sea</a>. Its marginal seas include the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>, the Baltic, the North Sea, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-mexico">Gulf of Mexico</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Atlantic Ocean” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Pan-Arabism, in Arabic al-waḥda al-ʿarabiyya, “Arab unity”, espouses the unification of all Arab people in a single nation-state made up of the Arab countries of West Asia and North Africa, from the <b>Atlantic Ocean</b> to the Arabian Sea.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/pan-arabism">Pan-Arabism · Core Ideas</a></li></ul></section>
